  "query_preview": "..\nTonight to Saturday: The dry and mild weather remain most \ndominant until late next week. The ridging building over the \nregion this weekend is dampened by a system tracking into B.C. \nMonday, before rebuilding into Thursday. Expect variable mid to \nhigh clouds. Southwest winds increase over the basin Monday PM \nwith gusts of 15-20 mph. A system moves in between Friday and \nnext Saturday. This brings a threat of showers to the Cascades \nFriday, expanding to northern and eastern WA and ID Friday \nnight and Saturday; PoPs are largely 20%, with 30% around the \nmountains. Winds are also forecast to increase for next Friday \nand Saturday. The forecast currently shows gusts strongest \nFriday out of the west at 25-35 mph, strongest over central WA.\n\nLows are mostly in the 40s, some 50s near Wenatchee, deep basin\nand L-C Valley and upper 30s mountain valleys. Highs through \nThursday are forecast to be in the 70s to mid-80s, with Monday \nthe warmest day. Friday is forecast to see slightly cooler \nreadings, in the upper 60s and 70s. Then next Saturday highs are\nforecast to be in the upper 50s to 60s. However there is a \ngreater uncertainty by then with models showing a 10 degrees \nspread between 25th and 75th percentile; some solutions show\nhighs barely getting into the 50s and others push highs into \nthe 70s. Suffice to say, confidence in those details lower than \naverage. /Solveig",
